1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,600
Organisations that pay people in bitcoin often approve in a chat message,

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:10,000
pay from a wallet screen, and keep a screenshot as proof.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,400
When the wallet's answer is lost mid-payment, nobody knows whether the money moved.

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:20,000
So people retry and risk paying twice, or wait and leave someone unpaid.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,800
Orynt sits in front of a Lightning wallet you already run. It never holds your funds.

6
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:32,000
It decides who may pay, sends exactly once, and keeps the proof.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The payments you will see run on Bitcoin regtest: a local test chain with worthless coins.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,100
An operator pastes Bob's invoice.

9
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:48,000
Orynt decodes it and shows amount, network, expiry and the issuing node.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,800
Default policy: one approver up to 100,000 sats, two above.

11
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:57,000
The maker cannot approve their own payout. Operators and API keys cannot approve.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,400
The approver signs the exact payment with a key created in their browser.

13
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:07,000
Change any field afterwards and the signature stops counting.

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,200
Execute: Orynt first reserves a single-use send in PostgreSQL,

15
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:15,000
then asks Alice's LND node to pay.

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Settled with proof in hand: the preimage matches the invoice's payment hash.

17
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Only then does Orynt call a payout settled.

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,200
Bob's node independently shows the invoice paid, with the same preimage.

19
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:37,000
Excerpt of the recorded demo:full run.

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,500
Now the failure that causes double payments.

21
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:47,000
In this run we inject a fault: the answer is lost after LND accepted the payment.

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,800
Orynt does not call it failed. The state is unknown, and Send is switched off

23
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:56,000
with the reason written out in plain words.

24
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,800
A second send is refused with HTTP 409: the first attempt may already have paid.

25
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:07,000
Reconciling by payment hash finds LND's own record: settled, one payment.

26
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,100
The payout settles from the wallet's own record.

27
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:16,000
The receipt says it was settled after an uncertain moment.

28
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,200
We also killed the API process with SIGKILL right after LND accepted a payment.

29
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:28,000
After restart: unknown, never failed. Reconciled to settled. One payment.

30
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:34,900
A standalone command verifies the receipt offline: approvals, preimage, audit chain.

31
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:41,000
Verified, exit code 0, and --require-real would refuse a simulator receipt.

32
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,500
Change a single character of the receipt.

33
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:51,000
Rejected, exit code 1.

34
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,100
Limits today: regtest only, LND only, application-level enforcement,

35
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:59,000
no human audit, no users. Working title, not a launched product.
